WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.780
It sometimes comes as a bit of a surprise
to discover that certain movies have their

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:11.289
own sequels or prequels — but finding out
they're part of a whole trilogy can be downright

00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:12.740
astounding.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:19.090
From underrated classics to straight-to-video
monstrosities, these are a few of those movies.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.039
The XXX series is all about explosions, fights,
and tacky one-liners.

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:26.141
"Welcome to the Xander Zone!"

00:00:26.141 --> 00:00:30.580
The first movie cashed in on Vin Diesel's
Fast and Furious street cred, giving him the

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.600
title role as one-note badass Xander "XXX"
Cage.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.539
And although there's plenty on offer here
for action fans, the prefab script kinda lets

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:42.000
the rest of the movie down.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.500
XXX: State of the Union's switch from Diesel
to Ice Cube might have seemed ill-advised,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.920
but Cube's charisma and sense of wonder as
criminal mastermind-turned-secret agent Darius

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.780
Stone basically carries this absurd example
of popcorn cinema.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:04.500
And sure, the talents of Sam Jackson and Willem
Dafoe are pretty much wasted — but so what?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.230
Nobody is going to see a movie called XXX:
State of the Union for dramatic nuance.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:13.610
Diesel then returned to the franchise for
the belated third installment, 2017's XXX:

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:19.840
Return of Xander Cage, and the results were…
well, they were more or less exactly what

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.000
you'd expect.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.740
Directed by Park Chan-wook, Oldboy first stole
the spotlight when it won the Grand Prix at

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:32.670
Cannes, introducing a whole lot of people
to Korean cinema and stunning audiences around

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.670
the world.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:39.670
It also set the gold standard for hallway
fights, and featured the octopus-eating scene

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.479
of all octopus-eating scenes.

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:47.759
The movie also inspired a sadly inferior sequel
that just couldn't compare to the epic tragedy

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:49.159
of the original film.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:53.159
Still, if you want to explore the twisted
mind of Park Chan-wook even further, then

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:58.549
you're in luck, because Oldboy is actually
the second installment in the director's so-called

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:00.610
"vengeance trilogy."

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:06.580
This loosely thematic saga began in 2002 with
Sympathy for Mr. Vengeance, the story of a

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.650
man seeking revenge for the death of his daughter.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:14.540
Then Oldboy was released in 2003, the story
of a man who sets out to bring down the people

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.170
who imprisoned him for 15 years.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:21.970
Park finished his trilogy with Lady Vengeance,
the story of a woman who hunts down the man

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.530
who committed the crime for which she was
framed.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.970
Obviously, the trilogy revolves largely around
the concept of vengeance, and most notably

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:34.890
the idea that getting even with your enemies
never really solves your problems.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:42.450
In Park's world, revenge is a messy, bloody,
and murky business, and even if the protagonists

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:48.010
succeed in finding vengeance, they're never
much better off at the end of these movies.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:56.150
[screaming, breaking down and singing]

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:02.280
And while each film explores this in slightly
different ways, the vengeance trilogy is still

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.810
a must-watch series for fans of Korean movies,
gore-soaked cinema, and stories that aspire

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.580
to the bloody heights of Shakespeare.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.480
Coming off the back of his starring role in
Sons of Anarchy, Taylor Sheridan decided to

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.690
write Sicario, a brutal screenplay about America's
war on drugs and the nightmarish violence

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.880
along the U.S.-Mexican border.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:30.670
Eventually, the script picked up an incredible
cast as well as the nigh on unbeatable pairing

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:34.980
of director Denis Villeneuve and cinematographer
Roger Deakins.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:40.400
Inevitably, when Sicario hit theaters in 2015,
the critics were floored.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.260
Sheridan's next project was the equally impressive
Hell or High Water, a modern-day Western that

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.650
follows two West Texas brothers as they rob
banks in one economically desolate town after

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:51.650
another.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:56.430
The script earned Sheridan an Oscar nomination,
as well as one for Jeff Bridges for his turn

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.360
as a surly Texas Ranger.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:03.120
A year later, Sheridan made his directorial
debut with Wind River, a movie set in the

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.930
snowy wilderness of Wyoming, where a hunter
and an FBI agent attempt to solve the murder

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.230
of a Native American woman.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.900
While there aren't any individual characters
linking these films together, Sheridan has

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.000
described his films as a "frontier trilogy."

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.660
Talking with the Associated Press, Sheridan
said:

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:26.100
"[These movies are] an exploration of the
modern American frontier and how much has

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.820
changed, and how much hasn't changed, and
the consequences of assimilation that still

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.840
reverberate today."

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:38.470
To that end, Sheridan's frontier trilogy is
very much an examination of the secret struggles

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:44.230
and brutal realities of living in the rural
U.S.; a series about the forgotten people

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.760
of North America and their desperate fight
for survival.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:54.400
Thanks to Guillermo del Toro's love for all
things weird and wonderful, the world has

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:01.720
been gifted with some truly incredible films,
such as Pan's Labyrinth and The Shape of Water.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.820
But before del Toro was known as the monster
maestro, he struggled with a little creature

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.830
feature called Mimic.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:13.900
Del Toro had a very specific creative vision
for the movie, but the studio wanted something

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:18.560
different, and the resulting film is… well,
let's just say it's not one of the director's

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.560
best.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.500
Still, it has its moments, and the premise
— which involves giant cockroaches impersonating

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.220
human beings — is at least pretty cool.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:34.010
While Mimic was anything but a box office
success, it must have had that certain something,

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:38.590
because somebody at Dimension Films then decided
it had franchise potential.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:45.510
Mimic 2 was released direct to DVD in 2001,
and played out like an ickier version of Beauty

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.510
and the Beast.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:50.940
The sequel followed an entomologist from the
first film who's being stalked by a jealous,

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:56.130
humanoid insect that wants her for, uh, romantic
purposes.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:01.310
Interestingly, the 2003 straight-to-video
follow-up, Mimic 3: Sentinel, borrows the

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:06.910
plot from Rear Window by trapping its voyeuristic
hero inside an apartment, watching the world

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:08.320
through his camera.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.160
His spying soon turns into sleuthing, however,
when he realizes there's something strange

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:15.110
happening across the street...

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:19.320
something that might just involve some very
hungry bugs.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.460
Incredibly, this unlikely trilogy capper actually
did pretty well with the few critics who saw

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:25.460
it.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:30.060
But then again, who wouldn't want to watch
an Alfred Hitchcock movie with killer cockroaches?

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:37.139
It's a scientific fact that sharks are the
scariest animals on the planet, so it should

00:06:37.139 --> 00:06:42.420
come as no surprise that Open Water thrilled
audiences at Sundance — and subsequently

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:48.330
scared up $54 million against a $500,000 production
budget.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.830
This aquatic indie was directed by husband
and wife filmmakers Chris Kentis and Laura

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:58.780
Lau, and followed an unlucky couple who become
stranded in the Great Barrier Reef, desperately

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.840
trying to avoid becoming sushi for the sharks.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.300
With such an impressive showing at the box
office, it was only a matter of time before

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:12.200
a sequel came swimming along — but what's
weird about Open Water 2: Adrift is that there's

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:14.630
not a single shark in sight.

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:15.900
That's right.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.169
No sharks.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:22.080
Originally titled just Adrift, the movie wasn't
supposed to have anything to do with the movie

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.020
by Kentis and Lau, but some executive came
along and stuck the name Open Water on it.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:33.360
The sequel follows a bunch of genuine idiots
who jump off their yacht to go swimming, only

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.600
to find they've forgotten to lower the ladder
first.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.419
Stranded in the ocean, our forgetful heroes
then have to desperately try to get back on

00:07:41.419 --> 00:07:42.419
board.

00:07:42.419 --> 00:07:47.610
Then came Open Water 3: Cage Dive, released
straight to video by Lionsgate.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:52.010
It focuses on a group of Americans who go
— you guessed it — go cage diving off

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.650
the coast of Australia.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:58.250
This time, the sharks actually show up, and
soon enough these scuba divers are little

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:59.780
more than fish food.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:04.590
Still, although they at least got that part
right, it goes without saying that Open Water

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.639
3 isn't exactly a masterpiece.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.460
Source Code director Duncan Jones spent 15
years working on his passion project, a sci-fi

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.780
noir called Mute.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:22.210
Despite a myriad of personal and professional
problems that slowed him down, Jones soldiered

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:26.610
on and finally finished the movie, which was
released in 2018.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:31.770
Mute follows a silent bartender in the near
future who embarks on a blood-soaked quest

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.810
to find his missing girlfriend.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.900
Based on the trailer, you'd never guess this
was the second installment of a trilogy.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:44.700
According to Jones, however, Mute takes place
in the same universe as Moon, his critically

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.510
acclaimed space drama about a lonely astronaut
on an isolated moon base.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:56.150
Jones calls his cinematic world "the Moon-iverse,"
and once promised a third film would eventually

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:57.970
come along to complete the trilogy.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:03.130
In 2020, however, Jones revealed that he was
having trouble funding part three, and decided

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:07.480
to make it a graphic novel instead, with the
hopes that it may eventually become a movie

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:08.680
in the future.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:13.930
Madi : Once Upon a Time in the Future is the
story of two mercenary sisters on the run

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.390
and was fully-funded through a Kickstarter
campaign.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.920
Only time will tell if it gets the cinematic
treatment.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:27.100
A true classic of the 1980s, The Lost Boys
is basically The Goonies meets Dracula.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:31.390
Directed by Joel Schumacher, the film follows
two brothers who move to the city of Santa

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:35.180
Carla, otherwise known as the murder capital
of the world.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:39.390
Naturally, they have a difficult time settling
into their new home, especially since they're

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:43.580
welcomed to the neighborhood by a gang of
motorcycle-riding vampires — including a

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:52.460
fanged-up Kiefer Sutherland and an undead
Bill on a wholly different kind of adventure.

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:53.710
"Excellent!"

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:58.420
Despite finding considerable success at the
box office, The Lost Boys laid dormant for

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.260
over 20 years.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:05.200
For a brief moment, there was talk of a sequel
titled The Lost Girls, but unfortunately that

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.570
idea never came to pass.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:12.540
Instead, in 2008, we got the Lost Boys: The
Tribe, a movie that basically rehashed the

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:14.149
plot of the first film.

00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:18.670
It's all very familiar: New kids move into
town, face an evil vampire, and when things

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:22.920
start getting freaky, they turn to a vampire
hunter played by Corey Feldman.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:28.990
Of course, with such a stunningly unoriginal
plot, it should come as no surprise to find

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:34.190
that The Tribe has an impressive zero percent
rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:39.770
While the Lost Boys: The Thirst has the same
awful rating, it at least tries a little harder

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.480
in the plot department.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:47.320
Released in 2010, the third movie in the trilogy
follows Feldman as aged vampire slayer Edgar

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:52.700
Frog, who sets out to stop a group of vampires
from using a demonic drug to transform partygoers

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:54.649
into undead monsters.

00:10:54.649 --> 00:10:59.649
Of course, the third entry can't hold a crucifix
to the original film, even with a more interesting

00:10:59.649 --> 00:11:04.550
premise — so maybe it's time for Warner
Bros. to stake this saga in the heart.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:10.960
Written by Quentin Tarantino and directed
by Robert Rodriguez, From Dusk till Dawn is

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.860
a bona fide cult classic.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.260
Released in 1996, the movie stars Tarantino
and George Clooney as criminal brothers who

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:22.300
escape to Mexico after robbing and killing
several people.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.760
They plan to spend the night hiding out at
a strip club before meeting their contact

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:31.510
at dawn, but what they don't know is that
the bar is staffed entirely by vampires.

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:37.029
Soon enough, all hell breaks loose in a full-blown
orgy of blood and violence.

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:42.450
As well regarded as From Dusk till Dawn is,
however, nobody seems to remember its two

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:43.450
sequels.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:48.550
For one thing, From Dusk till Dawn boasts
an amazing cast of '90s stars, but of its

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:54.670
many lead actors, only Danny Trejo returned
for 1999's From Dusk till Dawn 2: Texas Blood

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:55.670
Money.

00:11:55.670 --> 00:12:00.430
Instead of Clooney and Tarantino, the movie
stars Robert Patrick and Duane Whitaker.

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:04.959
It also suffers for lacking the talents of
Tarantino and Rodriguez behind the camera,

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:08.550
and, unsurprisingly, ended up being released
directly to video.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:13.910
From Dusk till Dawn 3: The Hangman's Daughter,
which got a direct to video release in 2000,

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:17.030
is actually a prequel set in the early 1900s.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:22.260
It features even less star power than the
second one, but hey, at least Danny Trejo

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:23.990
is still there.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.241
That guy really needs to learn how to say
no to people.

00:12:28.099 --> 00:12:34.589
2017's Call Me By Your Name was a huge success
as far as independent movies go, gaining heaps

00:12:34.589 --> 00:12:39.970
of critical acclaim and four Oscar nominations,
one of which it won.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:45.839
This wistful, romantic love story starred
Armie Hammer and Timothée Chalamet in a whirlwind

00:12:45.839 --> 00:12:50.330
summertime romance between two men with something
of an age difference.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.740
"I would kiss you if I could."

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:58.760
Still, despite attracting a little controversy
in that regard, Call Me By Your Name was a

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:02.620
resounding triumph for director Luca Guadagnino.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:07.880
But what you might not know is that it's actually
the third installment in a trilogy.

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:12.490
Guadagnino has often expressed his interest
in the desire that human beings feel for one

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:18.180
another, and he spent the better part of a
decade crafting a trilogy of narratively unrelated

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.690
films revolving around that theme.

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:26.870
The first was I Am Love in 2009, which starred
Tilda Swinton as the wife of a wealthy industrialist

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:28.970
who has a passionate affair with a chef.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.400
Then, in 2015, came A Bigger Splash, which
also starred Swinton, this time as a rock

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:39.420
star torn between past and present lovers,
even as her current boyfriend is tempted by

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.000
a younger woman.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:46.521
Call Me By Your Name is the only film in Guardagnino's
Desire Trilogy not to feature Swinton, but

00:13:46.521 --> 00:13:51.779
it shares thematic elements and picturesque
Italian settings with those first two installments.

00:13:51.779 --> 00:13:56.610
Whether that third movie marks the end of
Guadagnino's dalliances with desire, however,

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:57.610
is yet to be seen.

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:04.100
William Friedkin's 1971 movie The French Connection
is an absolute classic.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:09.600
The gritty story of New York detective Popeye
Doyle and his attempt to thwart a heroin-smuggling

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:15.930
operation, Friedkin's picture influenced crime
cinema for decades to come and is still considered

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:18.529
a must-see for cinephiles today.

00:14:18.529 --> 00:14:24.050
But despite The French Connection's enduring
popularity, hardly anybody has seen French

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:28.600
Connection II, and most people don't even
know there was a third movie in the series,

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:29.600
too.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:33.519
Gene Hackman returned as Popeye Doyle for
French Connection II, with John Frankenheimer

00:14:33.519 --> 00:14:35.779
taking over directorial duties.

00:14:35.779 --> 00:14:40.330
It follows Popeye as he travels to France
in pursuit of Alan Charnier, the drug smuggler

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:42.770
who escaped at the end of the first film.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:47.529
Fernando Rey is the only other cast member
besides Hackman to return for the sequel,

00:14:47.529 --> 00:14:52.089
which also features a series of harrowing
scenes of addiction and recovery as Doyle

00:14:52.089 --> 00:14:57.110
is captured by Charnier and injected with
heroin as an interrogation tactic.

00:14:57.110 --> 00:15:03.149
By the time Charnier lets him go, Popeye Doyle
is a full-blown addict, and must go cold turkey

00:15:03.149 --> 00:15:05.490
in a French police station holding cell.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:08.170
It's not exactly a barrel of laughs.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:12.550
The third film to pick up the story is called
Popeye Doyle, and stars Ed O'Neill as the

00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:13.790
title character.

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:18.720
The movie was made for television, and seemed
designed as the launching point for a television

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.800
show, but the spin-off series never really
happened.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:27.019
Still, even if it was more of a TV pilot,
Popeye Doyle was clearly established as a

00:15:27.019 --> 00:15:31.410
further story following the same character
from the first two films — so it certainly

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:33.690
counts as the third installment in the trilogy.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:41.089
One of the all-time great monster movies,
The Creature from the Black Lagoon introduced

00:15:41.089 --> 00:15:46.560
the world to the Gill-man, the scaliest member
of the Universal Monsters roster.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:52.279
And like his equally iconic cohorts, old Gilly
inspired a franchise that would inspire both

00:15:52.279 --> 00:15:55.920
fear and pity in this prehistoric fiend.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:16:00.240
The whole concept of the Gill-man began when
producer William Alland attended a dinner

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.170
party at Orson Welles' house.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:06.399
There, he met a cinematographer who told the
story of a reptilian monster that lived in

00:16:06.399 --> 00:16:10.100
the Amazon and occasionally kidnapped native
women.

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:15.800
This bizarre anecdote inspired a spark in
Alland, who went on to make that famous first

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:20.680
film — which is about a troop of explorers
who travel to South America and encounter

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:24.230
an amphibious beast with a thing for women
in bathing suits.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:29.680
The movie was released in 3-D, a revolutionary
innovation at the time, and turned out to

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:31.980
be an immediate hit.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:37.019
And before production had even finished, Universal
was already setting up the next entry in a

00:16:37.019 --> 00:16:38.340
new series.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:43.779
The follow-up was titled Revenge of the Creature,
and, in this film, the Gill-Man is captured,

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:47.680
put on display, and tortured by scientists.

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:53.450
Soon enough, however, he escapes, and our
fishy friend decides to go looking for revenge.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:58.870
Things then get super weird in the final film,
The Creature Walks Among Us.

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:03.810
In this entry, the Gill-man is set on fire,
and most of his scales are burned off.

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:09.179
Then, after undergoing surgery, the creature
comes out looking incredibly human-like.

00:17:09.179 --> 00:17:13.100
The rest of the film follows the Gill-man
as he tries to adjust to the human world,

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:16.589
all the while dreaming of his home beneath
the water.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:18.589
It's… not great.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:26.429
The 2010 sci-fi action film Skyline is less
a piece of cinema and more a truly wild, over-the-top

00:17:26.429 --> 00:17:27.720
thrill ride.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:32.529
It stars Eric Balfour and Scottie Thompson
as Jarrod and Elaine, a married couple with

00:17:32.529 --> 00:17:38.330
a baby on the way, who attend a friend's birthday
party — as aliens suddenly invade Los Angeles.

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:42.789
The film has a truly strange ending, too,
in which Jarrod and Elaine are captured and

00:17:42.789 --> 00:17:48.000
taken aboard an alien ship, where Jarrod's
brain is extracted from his body and placed

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:53.369
into a biomechanical alien body… you know,
for reasons.

00:17:53.369 --> 00:17:57.859
But Jarrod's brain suddenly glows red instead
of blue like the other alien drones, which

00:17:57.859 --> 00:18:01.809
apparently means he has retained his memories
and personality.

00:18:01.809 --> 00:18:06.809
The movie ends with the alien Jarrod protecting
his still-human wife and unborn child from

00:18:06.809 --> 00:18:08.200
the other aliens.

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:13.710
Incredibly, things get even stranger in Beyond
Skyline, a follow-up flick in which Elaine's

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:19.009
pregnancy is accelerated — as is the growth
of the daughter to whom she gives birth.

00:18:19.009 --> 00:18:23.029
Along the way, a resistance movement against
the aliens begins to come together, including

00:18:23.029 --> 00:18:27.960
other alien drones who manage to maintain
their inner humanity, just as Jarrod did.

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:33.669
The third movie in the trilogy, Skylines,
finds humanity and their half-human allies

00:18:33.669 --> 00:18:38.460
finally managing to defeat the aliens ten
years later, under the leadership of Jarrod

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.519
and Elaine's fully-grown 10-year-old daughter.

00:18:41.519 --> 00:18:46.489
Director Liam O'Donnell helmed all three movies,
so there does appear to be some kind of artistic

00:18:46.489 --> 00:18:50.279
vision at work here… no matter how little
sense it seems to make.

